# Remote Access with Tailscale

## Overview

Tailscale was added to the homelab to provide secure remote access to internal resources without exposing management interfaces or internal services directly to the public Internet.

The Linux File Server acts as a **Tailscale subnet router**, allowing authorized remote devices to reach systems inside the server network.

This provides remote access to internal resources such as:

- DC01
- Internal DNS
- Linux services
- Other systems on the advertised subnet

---

## Subnet Router Design

The Linux File Server operates on:

```text
IP Address: 10.10.20.20
VLAN: 20 - Servers
```

The server advertises the following internal subnet through Tailscale:

```text
10.10.20.0/24
```

This allows remote Tailscale-connected devices to reach systems on the server VLAN without installing Tailscale on every internal device.

The traffic path is:

```text
Remote Device
     |
     v
Tailscale Network
     |
     v
Linux File Server
10.10.20.20
     |
     v
Subnet Route
10.10.20.0/24
     |
     v
Internal Server Network
```

---

## Linux IP Forwarding

Because the Linux server forwards traffic between Tailscale and the internal server network, IPv4 forwarding was enabled.

The following command enabled forwarding:

```bash
sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1
```

To make the configuration persistent, the following setting was written to:

```text
/etc/sysctl.d/99-tailscale.conf
```

with:

```text
net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1
```

The configuration was then reloaded using:

```bash
sudo sysctl --system
```

