## Remote DNS Testing

The same remote system was also used to test internal DNS resolution.

The remote computer was using the public DNS server:

```text
8.8.8.8
```

When querying:

```text
ad.homelab.com
```

through public DNS, the lookup failed.

This is expected because `ad.homelab.com` is an internal DNS zone hosted by DC01 and is not available through public DNS.

The internal DNS server was then queried directly:

```text
nslookup ad.homelab.com 10.10.20.10
```

The query successfully returned:

```text
Name:    ad.homelab.com
Address: 10.10.20.10
```

This demonstrates the difference between:

- Public DNS resolution
- Private/internal DNS resolution
- Network reachability
- DNS server selection

---

## Why the First DNS Query Failed

The failed lookup did not indicate a network connectivity problem.

The remote system could already reach DC01 by IP address, which confirmed that the Tailscale route was working.

The issue was DNS selection.

The normal lookup path was:

```text
Remote Windows PC
      |
      v
DNS Server 8.8.8.8
      |
      v
Public DNS
      |
      v
ad.homelab.com not found
```

The successful lookup path was:

```text
Remote Windows PC
      |
      v
Tailscale
      |
      v
Linux Subnet Router
      |
      v
DC01 DNS
10.10.20.10
      |
      v
ad.homelab.com
```

This was useful for separating a **routing problem** from a **DNS configuration problem**.

